### Revised Rule

The transformation involves two main steps:

1. **Identify the "main" object**: This is the largest contiguous region of the non-background color (i.e., the color that is not the most frequent background color, which is 1 in example 1, 0 in example 2, and 8 in the test). In each example, this object is a vertical bar or line that runs from top to bottom, often with a horizontal segment at the bottom (like a "T" or "L" shape). Specifically, it is the object that touches the bottom edge of the grid and extends upward.

2. **Remove all other objects of the same color**: Any other pixels of that same non-background color that are not part of the main object are changed to the background color. The main object itself remains unchanged.

In practice, this means:
- Find all connected components (4-connected) of the non-background color.
- Determine which component touches the bottom row of the grid (i.e., has at least one cell in the last row). That component is the "main" object.
- For every cell that is the non-background color but not in the main object, set it to the background color.

This rule consistently explains both training examples: in example 1, the main 6 object is the vertical bar on the right that touches the bottom; in example 2, the main 3 object is the vertical bar on the right that touches the bottom. All other stray 6 or 3 pixels are removed.

