### Revised Rule

The transformation extracts a subgrid that contains the "innermost" object(s) and the objects that are directly connected to it via adjacency (including diagonals), while preserving their relative positions. Specifically:

1. Identify all distinct objects (contiguous regions of the same color, where color ≠ background). Background is defined as the most frequent color in the input grid (which appears as the large surrounding area). In these examples, the background is 8 in example 1, 1 in example 2, and 2 in example 3.

2. Find the object that is most "central" in the sense of being surrounded by other objects or being the deepest nested. This is determined by locating the object that has the maximum number of other objects adjacent to it (including diagonally touching). If multiple objects tie, choose the one with the smallest area.

3. From that central object, collect all objects that are adjacent to it (again including diagonals) and also include any objects that are adjacent to those (i.e., the connected component of objects touching the central object via adjacency). This forms a cluster of objects.

4. Compute the bounding box of this cluster (the minimal rectangle that contains all pixels of all objects in the cluster). Crop the input grid to this bounding box.

5. Within the cropped subgrid, replace all background pixels (the original background color) with the color of the central object, except for pixels that belong to any object in the cluster (they keep their original color).

6. Finally, if the resulting grid has any rows or columns that consist entirely of the central object's color (which is now the new background), remove those rows/columns from the top, bottom, left, or right until no such border rows/columns exist.

This rule consistently produces the outputs for all three training examples.

