### Revised Rule

The transformation identifies all connected regions of the background color (the most frequent color in the input grid, which is the dominant background). For each such background region, we examine the set of distinct non-background colors that are adjacent to it (via 4‑connectivity). If the region is adjacent to exactly one non‑background color, then the entire background region is recolored to that color. If it is adjacent to multiple distinct non‑background colors, the region is recolored to the color that appears most frequently among the adjacent non‑background cells; ties are broken by choosing the smallest color value. After recoloring, the original non‑background objects remain unchanged (they keep their original colors). This process effectively “floods” each background region with the color of the dominant neighboring object.

