The transformation identifies all distinct objects (connected components of non-zero color, 4-connected) in the input. The object with the largest total area (sum of pixels) becomes the "canvas color" (background) for the output. All other objects are transformed into solid rectangles (filled with their original color) and placed onto the canvas. The position and size of each rectangle are determined by taking the bounding box of the object in the input, scaling its coordinates and dimensions down by a factor of 2/3 (i.e., multiplying by 2/3 and rounding to the nearest integer), and then aligning the top-left corner of the scaled bounding box to the grid. The output grid dimensions are the maximum extents among these scaled rectangles, plus a border of at least one cell of the canvas color around all rectangles (effectively, the canvas extends to enclose all rectangles with a margin). The canvas is filled entirely with the canvas color, then each rectangle is drawn (overwriting the canvas) as a solid block of the object's color within its scaled bounding box. Objects that are single isolated cells (area 1) are ignored entirely and do not produce rectangles.

