### Revised Rule

The transformation identifies vertical separator columns of a specific color that are not the background color. The background color is defined as the color of the top-left cell. The rule proceeds as follows:

1. Identify the background color \( B \) as the value of the top-left cell.
2. Find all columns where every cell in that column has the same color \( C \), and \( C \neq B \). These are candidate separator columns.
3. Among these, select the separator color \( S \) as the color that appears most frequently among these candidate columns (if multiple, choose the smallest such color value).
4. Identify all columns where every cell is exactly color \( S \). These are the true separator columns.
5. If there are at least two such separator columns, consider the region between the first two separators (columns strictly between them). Let the left separator be at index \( L \) and the right separator at index \( R \). Extract the subgrid consisting of all rows and columns from \( L+1 \) to \( R-1 \) inclusive.
6. If there is exactly one separator column, extract all columns before it (from column 0 to \( L-1 \) inclusive).
7. If there are no separator columns, output the entire input grid unchanged.
8. After extraction, if the resulting subgrid contains any cells with color \( S \) (the separator color), replace them with the background color \( B \). This step cleans up any stray separator pixels that might appear inside the region due to noise or object overlap.

This rule correctly handles all training examples by focusing on the consistent separator color, extracting the region between the first two separators, and cleaning up any remaining separator color within the extracted region.

