More precisely:
1. Find all connected components (4-directionally) of nonzero colors. Each component is an object with a unique color and a set of cells.
2. For each object, compute its min_row (top) and max_row (bottom).
3. For each column in the grid, determine which objects intersect that column (i.e., have at least one cell in that column). Sort these objects by their min_row ascending.
4. Initialize an output grid of zeros with the same dimensions as the input.
5. For each column, starting from the top row, for each object intersecting that column in order of increasing min_row, set all cells in that column from min_row to max_row to the object's color, overwriting any previous color in that range.
6. This results in columns being filled with the color of the topmost object that covers each row segment.

