### Revised Rule

The transformation involves identifying "holes" (0 cells) that are completely surrounded by color 1 (foreground) in all four orthogonal directions (up, down, left, right) within the same contiguous region of 1s. Specifically, a hole is defined as a 0 cell that has at least one 1 in each of the four orthogonal directions, and all those 1s belong to the same connected component of 1s (i.e., they are part of the same object). For each such hole, we draw a 3×3 block of color 7 centered on the hole, overwriting existing colors, but keep the center cell as 0. If multiple blocks overlap, they combine (overwrites with 7). All other cells remain unchanged.

Note: In the training examples, some holes that are not fully surrounded (e.g., missing a 1 in one direction) do not trigger the block drawing. Also, holes that are adjacent to the border of the 1 object (i.e., not fully enclosed) are ignored. The rule explains why some interior 0s are ignored (they lack a 1 in some direction) and why blocks appear offset (they are centered on the hole, but if the hole is near the edge of the object, the block may extend beyond the object's original boundary, but still within the grid).

