The transformation identifies each distinct color that forms at least one vertical line (contiguous vertical segment of height ≥2, same color, in a single column). For each such color, we consider its "vertical line objects": each maximal vertical contiguous segment of that color in a column. For each row that intersects any vertical line object of that color, we determine the leftmost and rightmost columns where that color appears in that row (considering only cells that are part of any vertical line object of that color). Then, in that row, we fill all cells between that leftmost and rightmost column (inclusive) with that color, but only if the cell is currently background (0) or is already part of a vertical line object of that color (so we don't overwrite other vertical lines of different colors). Additionally, if a vertical line object of that color is adjacent horizontally (left or right) to a cell of a different color that is not part of any vertical line object, that adjacent cell is also changed to the vertical line's color, but only if it is directly adjacent and not separated by background. This effectively "expands" the vertical lines horizontally to fill the gaps between them in each row, while preserving other vertical lines of different colors.

