### Revised Rule

The transformation identifies the largest contiguous region of non‑background color (color 0) in the input. If there are multiple regions of the same maximal size, choose the one with the smallest color value. This region is considered the “main object.” All other pixels (including other objects and the outer border) are set to background (0). Then the bounding box of the main object is computed, and that bounding box is centered in the output grid, padding with the background color (0). If the bounding box is larger than the output grid dimensions, it is cropped to fit. Finally, the original background color (0) inside the main object is preserved, but the padding outside the bounding box is set to the original border color that surrounded the main object in the input (determined by the most frequent color among the 4‑connected neighbors of the main object’s pixels that are not part of the main object). If no such neighbor color exists, use color 0.

