### Revised Rule

The transformation identifies all solid rectangular blocks (objects) of size at least 2×2 in the input grid. For each such block, we compute its "source color" as the color of the single‑cell objects that lie directly above the block’s top row, within the same horizontal span (columns) as the block, but only considering those single cells that are isolated (i.e., not part of a larger object). If there is exactly one such single cell above the block, its color becomes the "target color". The block is then replicated downward, starting immediately below the original block, with a vertical gap equal to the block’s height minus one row, and repeated as many times as fits within the grid. Each replica is painted with the target color. If there is no qualifying single cell above the block, the block is not replicated.

More formally:
1. Find all connected components (objects) of the same color, ignoring the background (color 0 is not present here; background is a different color, e.g., 8 in example 1, 1 in example 3). For each object, compute its bounding box.
2. If the bounding box is exactly filled with that color (i.e., the object is a solid rectangle) and its height ≥ 2 and width ≥ 2, it is a candidate block.
3. For each candidate block, look at the row immediately above its top row. Within the same column range as the block, examine the cells. If there is exactly one cell in that range that is not part of any larger object (i.e., it is a single‑cell object of its own color, not touching any other cell of the same color horizontally or vertically), then its color is the target color.
4. Starting from the row directly below the block, paint a copy of the block (same shape) with the target color. Then skip a gap of (block height − 1) rows, and paint another copy, repeating until the bottom of the grid is reached. Overwrites may occur; later copies overwrite earlier ones if they overlap.
5. The original block remains unchanged.

