### Explanation
The rule fails on both training examples because it does not correctly produce the output grids. In Example 1, the background color is 8 (most frequent), and objects are colors 1, 3, 2, 4, 6. Their vertical extents: object 1 appears in rows 1-4 (4 rows), object 3 in rows 5-7 (3 rows), object 2 in rows 7-9 (3 rows), object 4 in rows 10-11 (2 rows), object 6 in row 12 (1 row). The rule would output a column with blocks: 1 (height 4), 3 (height 3), 2 (height 3), 4 (height 2), 6 (height 1), total rows 13. But the actual output has 22 rows and repeats some colors (e.g., 1 appears 5 times, 3 appears 5 times, etc.). The rule does not match the repetition pattern or total height.

In Example 2, background is 1 (most frequent), objects are 8, 2, 3. Vertical extents: object 8 appears in rows 1-4 (4 rows), object 2 in rows 5-9 (5 rows), object 3 in rows 10-14 (5 rows). The rule would output blocks: 8 (height 4), 2 (height 5), 3 (height 5), total 14 rows. But the actual output has 24 rows with many more repetitions (e.g., 8 appears 8 times, 2 appears 8 times, 3 appears 9 times). The rule fails to account for the actual heights in the output.

Thus, the rule does not correctly transform the training inputs into their outputs. It also lacks generality because it does not explain the specific repetition counts observed.

