### Revised Rule

The transformation involves reflecting all objects (contiguous regions of the same color, excluding color 1) across the central vertical line at column 13 (0-indexed, so column index 13 is the center). The background color is 4, but any non-1 color can be part of an object. The steps are:

1. Identify all objects in the input grid: connected components of the same color (using 4-way connectivity) where the color is not 1. The central column (col 13) is color 1 and is fixed; it is not part of any object and remains unchanged.
2. For each object, compute its bounding box (min row, max row, min col, max col). Determine its horizontal position relative to the central line:
   - If the object is entirely left of column 13 (max col < 13), it will be mirrored to the right side.
   - If entirely right of column 13 (min col > 13), mirrored to the left side.
   - If straddling column 13 (min col ≤ 13 ≤ max col), it is not moved (remains in place), but this case does not occur in the training examples.
3. To mirror an object:
   - Compute the horizontal reflection of its bounding box: 
     - For a left object, the new left column = 26 - max col, new right column = 26 - min col.
     - For a right object, the new left column = 26 - max col, new right column = 26 - min col.
   - The vertical position (rows) remains unchanged.
   - Place the object's pixels in the new bounding box, preserving their relative positions but mirrored horizontally (i.e., the leftmost pixel of the object becomes the rightmost pixel in the new box, etc.). This is equivalent to reflecting the object's shape across the central line.
4. The output grid starts as a copy of the input grid, but with all non-1 colors set to background (4) except the central column (which remains 1). Then, each mirrored object is drawn onto the output grid at its new position, overwriting the background. If two objects overlap in the output, the later one (order doesn't matter since objects are disjoint in input) may overwrite, but in practice they do not overlap.
5. Objects that are already symmetrically placed (i.e., their mirrored position coincides with their original position) remain unchanged.

This rule correctly handles both training examples by reflecting entire objects, not row segments.

