Revised Rule:
The grid consists of repeated rectangular blocks separated by rows and columns of zeros. Each block is a square region of non-zero cells surrounded by a border of zeros. Within each block, there is an inner shape (a smaller square of non-zero cells) that is surrounded by a border of a single color (the "frame" color). The transformation identifies a "key pixel" within each block: the pixel at the center of the inner shape (i.e., the geometric center of the inner square). In the top-left block, this key pixel has a certain value. The rule then propagates this value to the corresponding key pixel position in every other block across the grid, overriding whatever value was there originally. All other pixels remain unchanged.

More precisely:
1. Identify blocks: The grid is partitioned by zero rows and columns into contiguous non-zero rectangles. Each block has dimensions (block_height × block_width), consistent across the grid.
2. For each block, locate the inner shape: It is the largest contiguous region of non-zero cells inside the block, excluding the outer border of the block (which is zeros). The inner shape is itself a smaller square (e.g., 3×3) of a single color (the frame color) except possibly for one interior pixel that may differ.
3. The key pixel is the cell at the center of this inner shape. If the inner shape has odd dimensions, the center is unambiguous. In all examples, the inner shape is 3×3, so the center is the middle cell (row index 1, column index 1 within the inner shape).
4. Determine the value of the key pixel in the top-left block (the first block encountered in row-major order).
5. For every other block, set its key pixel to that same value.
6. All other cells, including the borders and the rest of the inner shape, remain as in the input.

