### Revised Rule

The transformation identifies specific "T-junction" patterns where a color-5 pixel is adjacent to exactly three color-5 neighbors in a shape resembling a "T" (i.e., three neighbors in three of the four cardinal directions, with the missing direction being either up, down, left, or right). At each such T-junction, if the pixel directly opposite the missing direction (i.e., two steps away in that direction) is color 4, then the central color-5 pixel is changed to color 2. More formally:

For each cell (r,c) with value 5, examine its four cardinal neighbors (up, down, left, right). Let the set of directions where the neighbor exists (within grid bounds) and has value 5 be D. If D has exactly 3 elements, then the missing direction is M. Let (r2,c2) be the cell two steps away from (r,c) in direction M (i.e., if M is up, then (r-2,c); down: (r+2,c); left: (r,c-2); right: (r,c+2)). If (r2,c2) is within bounds and has value 4, then set the value at (r,c) to 2.

This rule is applied to all cells independently (i.e., changes are based on the original input grid, not intermediate changes).

